+\subsection{The common two-dimensional input must not be averaged twice}
+For $d=2$, 76 six-loop finite MOM kernels have the independently measured
+combined contribution
+\begin{equation}
+ K_{76}=528.2946383532544,\qquad
+ \widehat\sigma_{76}=0.1373774929551.
+ \label{prec:known76}
+\end{equation}
+It is the sum of the primitive, one-subgraph and two-subgraph contributions
+in the \emph{finite scheme}.  It must not be confused with their separate
+MS-converted values.  Their retained component estimates are
+\begin{center}\begin{tabular}{lrr}
+\toprule Finite-kernel group & Mean & Sampling standard error\\\midrule
+Primitive, 10 classes & 718.7129769223 & 0.0414043283\\
+One-subgraph, 26 classes & -188.2890632769 & 0.1002179258\\
+Two-subgraph, 40 classes & -2.1292752922 & 0.0843482337\\
+\bottomrule\end{tabular}\end{center}
+The noisy production estimates of these 76 graphs are excluded from the
+final six-loop vector.  The retained $K_{76}$ is then added once to the
+physical coefficient; its error is propagated once as a common input.
+The 28 six-loop classes with zero target weight remain exact zeros.
+Thus 291 nonzero six-loop target kernels are sampled for the new
+remainder.  Minimal samples retained for excluded strata are diagnostic
+only and are not used to reduce the error of $K_{76}$.
+
+In particular, the two new $d=2$ campaign estimates are correlated by
+$\operatorname{Cov}_{76}=\widehat\sigma_{76}^{\,2}$ even though their new
+sample streams are independent.  Treating this shared input as two
+independent measurements would underestimate the final error.
+There is no analogous carried numerical group in the $d=3$ estimator.
+The uncertainty in \eqref{prec:known76} also gives a floor for improving
+$d=2$ precision without recalculating that group.
+
+\subsection{Covariance propagation and consistency diagnostics}
+Write $\overline{\boldsymbol v}_{aL}$ for the mean vector of campaign
+$a\in\{A,B\}$ at loop $L$, and $S_{aL}$ for the sample covariance of
+its 64 replicate vectors.  Set
+\begin{equation}
+ \overline{\boldsymbol v}_L
+  =\tfrac12(\overline{\boldsymbol v}_{AL}
+                  +\overline{\boldsymbol v}_{BL}),\qquad
+ V_L=\tfrac14\left(\frac{S_{AL}}{64}+\frac{S_{BL}}{64}\right).
+ \label{prec:jetcov}
+\end{equation}
+If $J_L$ is the Jacobian of the complete coefficient vector $F_d$ with
+respect to $\boldsymbol v_L$, evaluated at the pooled mean, then
+\begin{equation}
+ \widehat V_C=\sum_{L=3}^{6}J_LV_LJ_L^{\mathsf T}
+       +\delta_{d,2}\widehat\sigma_{76}^{\,2}
+          \boldsymbol e_6\boldsymbol e_6^{\mathsf T}.
+ \label{prec:covariance}
+\end{equation}
+The independent loops have separate randomizations.  Within each loop,
+beta/mass and regulator-Taylor covariance are fully preserved.  The
+statistical unit is the independently scrambled \emph{replicate}, not
+an individual quasi-random point: the latter are not independent
+Monte Carlo observations \cite{PrecisionQMC}.
